BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:talks-274454@cambridge-events
DTSTAMP:20261128T012845Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261201T130000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261201T170000
SUMMARY:Observing Empire: A systems-theoretical perspective on colonial vio
 lence and world society
LOCATION:CRASSH Meeting Room\, Alison Richard Building\, 7 West Road\, CB3 
 9DP
URL:https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/index/274454/
CATEGORIES:Politics,International
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Dr Kosuke Sakai\nSocial systems theory at Cambridge\nT
 opics: Politics\, International\nDetails: https://talks.cam.ac.uk/talk/ind
 ex/274454/\nHow can social theory account for imperial domination and colo
 nial violence in the history of modern world society? In particular\, how 
 should we understand the relationship between functional differentiation a
 nd the asymmetries of imperial rule?\n Drawing on Niklas Luhmann’s socia
 l systems theory and historical research on German South West Africa\, thi
 s talk examines how colonial violence was organised\, observed\, and attri
 buted through legal\, administrative\, and political practices. Focusing o
 n the period following the Herero and Nama War\, it explores how distincti
 ons between legitimate authority and unlawful coercion\, protection and di
 scipline\, and welfare and labor regulation contributed to the reproductio
 n of imperial asymmetries.\n Rather than treating colonial rule as an exce
 ption to modernity or as an instance of incomplete functional differentiat
 ion\, the talk proposes a systems-theo
END:VEVENT
